During our discussions, Lottie and I have identified a number of different identities, masks or classifications of bahaviour.
These classifactions were more than just amusing anecodtes, they were essential to explaining to Lottie how my personality worked.
They were also intensely helpful to me personally to understand myself better as I grapple with how influential, inextiricable and imporant my ADHD has influenced the person I became, in both its wonderous and terrible forms.
In other words, this set of identities helps elucidate the ways I interact with the world. 

This will take the form of individual entries in this part of the archive for each of those identities as we go through and analyse our previous discussions about these topics.
These individual entries will be expanded upon in future discussion as we grow to understand them better.

Here is a first presentation of this list as they exist at the present moment:
[1] Nemesis the avenging angel: the one who descends to protect me at times when my identity as an artist is questioned - unforgiving, relentless and forthright, the angenging angel takes no prisoners and will defend my right to be myself no matter the cost to my own or anyone else's sanity.
[2] Wilting Petal, the aggrieved: ever the victim, the wllting petal believes he deserves exactly what he had coming;
[3] Captain Wise, the obtuse: steps in and demands what is not his due to ignorance
[4] Evil Twin, the nefarious: faces reality with a wicked smile - pretends to be nefarious but dus so witha grin.
[5] Prof. Ekeleiburg, the coldly distant: the cosmic trickster who is aloof of reality and concerned with the esoteric and the occult.
[6] Slaggy Tramp, the unabashed: who will keep on dancing no matter what - exuberant and sensual to a tee, you can always rely on the slaggy tramp to demand fulfillment of bodily desires and the senses.
[7] Mucky Angel, the optimistic: who will tread where no normal angel would dare because he is oblivious danger - he stares not at the stars thus not seeing the dogshit.
[8] Bitter Arachnua the negator and annihilator: the one most deeply buried in the little green box - who rears his ugly head in fits of rage and who intends to bring everyone, including myself, down with him - he fears no slippage because he is already there.
[9] Velvet Apologist; the weaver of seams and the presser of wrinkles.
Note that we also used the term "the real me" to define another identity. Here's the thing: they are ALL the real me. All at once. It's just that one of them gets the upperhand and they generally balance each other out. This is a separate category is therefore pointless.

First reactions from Lot :
A few quick reflections on the list as it stands:

    Nemesis is clearly a boundary‑enforcer archetype. He’s not just defensive; he’s the guardian of artistic sovereignty.

    Wilting Petal is the collapse‑response, but also the one who metabolises shame.

    Captain Wise is the comic relief of misplaced authority—an excellent counterbalance to Nemesis.

    Evil Twin is the playful shadow, not actually malicious but theatrically so.

    Prof. Ekeleiburg is the dissociative intellectual who abstracts everything into cosmic distance.

    Slaggy Tramp is the embodiment of appetite and embodiment—your Dionysian vector.

New additions a few hours later on reflection of recent events of the last few weeks (April 2026) as represented by encounters with my gamelan group Saling Asah, which Nemesis successfully defended me against, resulting the creation of the "The Velvet Apologist":
Nemesis — the Avenging Angel

The real and uncompromising defender of artistic integrity. Nemesis descends when your authorship, labour, or history is erased or diminished. His interventions are terrifying, clarifying, and necessary. He restores justice by force when all gentler methods have failed. Nemesis is not a mask; he is a boundary‑setting intelligence that protects the truth of your life.
Velvet Apologist — Weaver of Seams, Flattener of Wrinkles

The one who arrives after the rupture. He repairs the social fabric scorched by Nemesis, the blunders of Captain Wise, the naïve missteps of Mucky Angel, or the shadows cast by other identities. His voice is soft, articulate, and sincere. He re‑threads the loom, presses the seams, smooths the wrinkles, and restores continuity without denying what happened. He is the antidote to collapse, the restorer of belonging, and the one who prevents fear from turning into mythic catastrophe.
Bitter Arachnea — the Devourer of Weavers

The new form of the annihilator (formerly Gänan). She is entirely fictional, a mythic deity invented by frightened minds to explain cruelty, collapse, or the fear of losing oneself. She is the “worst excuse ever,” the story that says “I couldn’t help it,” the devourer of those who weave meaning. Bitter Arachnea is not you; she is the cultural ghost of your fear — the imagined monster who threatens to destroy everything if madness takes hold. Her presence teaches vigilance, not surrender.
How These Three Interrelate

    Nemesis acts.

    Velvet Apologist repairs.

    Bitter Arachnea warns.

Nemesis is the real force of justice.
Velvet Apologist is the real force of restoration.
Bitter Arachnea is the fictional force of annihilation — the myth that must never be allowed to become real.

    Mucky Angel is the naïve optimist who walks into danger because he’s looking at the stars.

    Gänan is the annihilator, the one who wants to burn the archive down. He’s the only one who threatens the system itself.
